\paragraph{Temporal context and modality choice.}
Information-theoretic reasoning suggests that hidden states (4096 to 5120 dimensions of internal computation) should dominate text features (384-dimensional sentence embeddings) purely by signal capacity. Both temporal context and modality choice significantly improve detection, but their relative magnitudes differ by scale. On R1-8B, the modality advantage (precision +22.4pp at $W{=}1$) exceeds the temporal improvement (+12.9pp from $W{=}1$ to $W{=}25$), establishing that modality choice matters more than temporal context at this scale. At 32B, the relative magnitudes depend on model and window size (\cref{sec:temporal}). Text probes benefit more from temporal context than HS probes because they start from a weaker single-step baseline and extract proportionally more from observing multi-step progressions. Safety commitment is a gradual process: the model progressively consolidates its intent over multiple reasoning steps rather than switching abruptly at a single token. Both modalities benefit from temporal context; for text probes, the improvement is universal across all four models. The practical implication is that temporal windowing ($W{=}3$ to $15$) remains the most robust single default for safety monitoring, as it provides guaranteed improvement regardless of modality, while the modality choice requires principled layer selection to realize its full potential.

\paragraph{Cross-model divergence within identical architecture.}
R1-32B and QwQ-32B share the same base architecture (Qwen-32B, 64 layers) but present opposite detection profiles (\cref{tab:32b_divergence}). R1-32B provides excellent lead time (+11.7 to +13.3 steps before commitment) with narrow coverage (25\% at the only significant window, $W{=}1$) and a strong margin over the position baseline (+14.2pp). QwQ-32B provides robust statistical significance (all 15 window sizes pass Holm-Bonferroni, 3/3 metrics each) with 74\% coverage, but lead time is negative ($-$1.2 to +1.9 steps) and the position baseline is competitive ($-$0.6pp difference). This divergence arises despite identical architecture and parameter count, making training method (R1 distillation vs.\ native reasoning) the likely differentiator. The practical implication is that per-model validation is essential: no single detection configuration generalizes even within the same architecture at the same scale. A monitoring system validated on QwQ-32B would fail to provide early warning on R1-32B, and one tuned for R1-32B's narrow operating window would achieve only 25\% coverage in deployment. An open question remains: whether the R1-32B dimensionality constraint ($51200 \times W$ features for $n{=}20$ traces) would resolve with more training data, or whether deep-layer signal at large $W$ is fundamentally sparse for distilled models.

\paragraph{Design implications for safety monitoring.}
