\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Layer selection as researcher degree of freedom.}
Layer selection is an underexamined methodological choice in the probing literature that can silently determine a study's conclusions. Our five-metric ablation (\cref{sec:layer-results}) demonstrates that a single metric component, the crossing rate, reverses the central finding of an HS vs.\ text comparison: at biased layers, only 5 of 12 tests survive Holm-Bonferroni correction, producing an apparent scale-dependent modality effect; at corrected layers, HS probes are significant on all four models across all three metrics (12/12 tests pass). The mechanism is general: crossing rate rewards representational volatility at shallow layers independently of discriminative power, and any composite metric that incorporates threshold-crossing frequency is susceptible to the same bias mechanism, whose practical impact depends on the specific task and model depth. This bias is not specific to safety probing. We hypothesize that any threshold-based detection study that uses crossing rate as a layer selection criterion, whether for sentiment, factuality, or toxicity, could produce analogous reversals. Validation on other probing tasks is needed to confirm generality. We recommend that probing studies report results under at least two complementary layer selection criteria, and that five-metric ablations (or at minimum, results at multiple candidate layers spanning shallow, middle, and deep positions) become standard practice. Even among unbiased metrics, the choice between threshold-based and threshold-free criteria affects layer selection: AUROC peaks at mid-depth layers (${\sim}50\%$) while precision peaks at deep layers ($>90\%$), further demonstrating that layer selection methodology is a critical researcher degree of freedom. The cost of ablation is modest: evaluating five metrics across all layers adds minutes to an experiment that takes hours. The alternative is that conclusions about representational structure reflect layer selection methodology rather than the phenomenon under study. The control task provides independent corroboration: R1-32B selectivity increases from 7.5pp at the biased layer (L10) to 19.7pp at the corrected layer (L63), confirming that the biased metric selected layers where positional features dominate over genuine safety structure.

